  "content": {
    "id": "p6-q6",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "The triangle $DEF$ has vertices $D(-2,1)$, $E(1,5)$ and $F(5,2)$.\n\nCalculate the gradient of each side, and hence show that $\\triangle DEF$ is a right-angled triangle.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "diagramPlacement": "beside-prompt",
    "answer": {
      "short": "$m_{DE}=\\frac43$, $m_{EF}=-\\frac34$, $m_{DF}=\\frac17$. Since $m_{DE}m_{EF}=-1$, $DE\\perp EF$ and $\\triangle DEF$ is right-angled at $E$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}m_{DE}&=\\frac{5-1}{1-(-2)}=\\frac43\\\\m_{EF}&=\\frac{2-5}{5-1}=-\\frac34\\\\m_{DF}&=\\frac{2-1}{5-(-2)}=\\frac17\\\\m_{DE}\\times m_{EF}&=\\frac43\\times\\left(-\\frac34\\right)=-1\\end{align*}$$\nThe gradients of $DE$ and $EF$ are negative reciprocals, so $DE\\perp EF$. Therefore $\\triangle DEF$ is right-angled at $E$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
